# M37 sanity check (PLAN_BROWSER_PERF_SECURITY.md §2.8, D46) — identical shape to bench.tflw, but
# opted into the trivial `bench` session (tflw.config), so every iteration pays for one extra
# per-iteration `sessionCache.ensure()` cache-hit call (D44) that bench.tflw's sessionless scenario
# never does. Compared against bench.tflw on the same echo-server.mjs harness to confirm that added
# call doesn't measurably regress throughput — the "cheap cache hit" claim in D44, checked, not
# just assumed.

test "profile bench (session)" as bench
  ramp to 30 users over 8s
  api GET /products
  expect status equals 200
  capture body[0].id as productId
  api POST /orders body { items: [{ productId: {productId}, quantity: 1 }] }
  expect status equals 201
  # `TF033` (tflw M60): a workload-bearing test's verdict comes only from its thresholds, so one is
  # required. This rung measures rather than gates, so the bound is deliberately loose — but not
  # vacuous: echo-server.mjs is a zero-latency local process, so an error here would most likely be
  # the `bench` session — which is the one thing this file exists to measure. See perf/README.md for
  # how this ladder is run and checked.
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
